1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:06,888
Two retirees with the same balance, the
same withdrawals and the same average

2
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:07,620
return.

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:11,120
One ran out of money in 1991.

4
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,330
So what decided it?

5
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:20,930
It is January 1966.

6
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:26,710
The first retiree starts with $1 million,
measured in 1966 dollars.

7
00:00:27,010 --> 00:00:32,730
They retire at the start of a hard decade
for markets, though nobody knows it yet.

8
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:39,458
By the end of this video, you will know
which ten years decided the fate of this

9
00:00:39,478 --> 00:00:45,290
retiree, and whether the same thing held
for every start year since 1928.

10
00:00:45,590 --> 00:00:51,330
The money sits in a portfolio of 60%
stocks and 40% bonds.

11
00:00:51,630 --> 00:00:57,050
The stocks are the Standard & Poor's 500
index, with dividends reinvested.

12
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:00,450
The bonds are 10-year US Treasury bonds.

13
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:04,850
Every January, the portfolio is rebalanced
back to that mix.

14
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:10,470
In the first year, the retiree withdraws
4% of the starting balance.

15
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:15,530
That is $40,000, in 1966 dollars.

16
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:21,731
After that, the withdrawal rises each year
with the previous year's inflation, so it

17
00:01:21,751 --> 00:01:23,910
always buys the same groceries.

18
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,690
In real terms, it never changes.

19
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:32,090
In nominal terms, the dollars of the day,
it climbs every year.

20
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:37,630
The plan runs for 30 years, from 1966
through 1995.

21
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:39,870
No taxes, no fees.

22
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:45,930
We keep the model this bare on purpose, so
that one thing can change at a time.

23
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:52,030
The data is US only, and this is history,
not a forecast.

24
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:54,890
Now meet the second retiree.

25
00:01:55,190 --> 00:02:00,947
The mirror retiree lives through the exact
same 30 annual returns, but in reverse

26
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:01,670
order.

27
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:08,590
The return of 1995 arrives first, and the
return of 1966 arrives last.

28
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:14,010
This retiree is illustrative: no one lived
through these years in this order.

29
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:19,723
Inflation stays in its real calendar
order, so both retirees take out the same

30
00:02:19,743 --> 00:02:21,050
dollars every year.

31
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:24,350
Here is the question this first part
answers.

32
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:30,330
If two people earn the same average
return, do they end up in the same place?

33
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:36,790
The portfolio of the 1966 retiree earned
an average return of 9.7%.

34
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:43,030
For the mirror retiree, the average is
also 9.7%.

35
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:44,330
Not close.

36
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:45,610
Identical.

37
00:02:45,710 --> 00:02:50,481
That is the geometric average, the
compound rate that turns the starting

38
00:02:50,501 --> 00:02:52,470
dollar into the ending dollar.

39
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:58,481
Multiplication does not care about order,
so the same years in any order give the

40
00:02:58,501 --> 00:02:59,501
same average.

41
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:06,710
Even the simple arithmetic average, adding
up the 30 returns and dividing by 30, is

42
00:03:06,862 --> 00:03:08,990
the same for both: 10.3%.

43
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:10,350
Same money in.

44
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:11,850
Same money out.

45
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:13,390
Same average.

46
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:16,830
The only thing that differs is the order.
